  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain-free and mild conditions will persist \nthrough the end of October\n\nAfter a prolonged stretch of blue sky and sunshine, we'll see\nincreasing clouds today as a moisture-deprived warm front lifts\nthrough the region. No rain is expected, but temperatures will\nbe several degrees cooler this afternoon compared to Tuesday.\n\nTemperatures will trend warmer on Thursday ahead of another \ncold front and then decrease a bit into the weekend. Saturday \nwill be similar to Wednesday with increasing clouds but no rain.\nDaytime and overnight temperatures will be +5 to +15 degrees \nabove average for this time of year through the weekend, \nproviding great conditions for trick or treat. Whether \nneighborhoods are hosting beggars night on Halloween or moving \nit earlier, mild and dry conditions will prevail. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Fire weather concerns may start to increase \nlater this week.\n\nIt has been almost two weeks since we've received any measurable\nrainfall across Central PA. The combination of drying fuels,\nincreasing leaf litter, and low humidity air could result in an\nelevated risk of wildfire spread Friday and Saturday. Friday\ncould be a bit breezy in the wake of a cold front. We will\ncontinue to be in close communication with the PA Bureau of\nForestry about potentially ramping up our wildfire safety\nmessaging if conditions warrant.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Trending wetter to start November\n\nAfter a rain-free second half of October, November will start \noff with a couple chances for rain. There remains some model \nuncertainty, but a trough should move through the central part \nof the United States early next week. This should deliver enough\nupper level support to generate some accumulating rainfall \nacross the Commonwealth. Climate Prediction Center outlooks lean\nwetter than average for the first couple of weeks in November, \nthough the signal is not super strong. Temperature trends favor \nabove normal temperatures with much higher confidence. Notably, \nthe monthly outlook for November begins to take on a classic El \nNino pattern with above normal precipitation favored across the \nsoutheast US up through the I-95 corridor and below normal \nprecipitation for the Great Lakes. This could set the stage for \nan active storm track bringing wet conditions to the Lower \nSusquehanna Valley while the northwest mountains continue their \ndry trend. Any locations that continue to be drier than average\nwill need to be monitored for an elevated wildfire risk as we\nhead deeper into fall.",
